format 1
adapter statements 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

statement radiation-meets-matter-s01
verdict: myth
statement: All radiation is the same.

explanation:
    Radiation comes in many kinds. Some can knock electrons out of atoms and some cannot.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s02
verdict: fact

statement:
    Radio waves, microwaves and visible light are radiation, but they do not have enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms.

explanation:
    They are non-ionizing. Ionizing radiation has enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s03
verdict: myth
statement: A sheet of paper stops gamma rays.

explanation:
    Paper stops alpha particles. Gamma rays pass through it and need thick, dense material to be reduced.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s04
verdict: fact
statement: Alpha particles cannot get through the outer layer of your skin.

explanation:
    Alpha particles are heavy and use up their energy over a very short distance.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s05
verdict: myth
statement: Radiation can spread onto you and stay on your skin.

explanation:
    Radiation does not get on or in people. Radioactive contamination, the material itself, is what can spread.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s06
verdict: fact
statement: Being near a source of radiation is not the same as being contaminated.

explanation:
    Exposure stops when the source is shielded or you move away. Contamination means radioactive material is on or in you.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s07
verdict: myth
statement: A Geiger counter that clicks must be next to a radioactive source.

explanation:
    Background radiation is always around, so a counter picks up some even with no source nearby.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s08
verdict: fact
statement: A dosimeter is used to find out how much radiation a person has been exposed to.

explanation:
    Dosimeters are worn to measure personal exposure.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s09
verdict: myth
statement: The becquerel, the gray and the sievert all measure the same thing.

explanation:
    The becquerel counts decays, the gray measures energy absorbed, and the sievert measures potential for harm.

statement radiation-meets-matter-s10
verdict: fact
statement: One becquerel is one decay per second.

explanation:
    The becquerel measures how active a source is.
